          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How can I tell from the tabular list that a code is a combination code rather than a plain one?",
          "answer": "Combination codes in ICD-10 carry two conditions, or a condition and a complication, combined into one code. This means that when a combination code is available, it is used instead of coding for the separate conditions individually. \n\nTo tell if a code is a combination code rather than a plain one, you can look for specific conventions in the tabular list. For instance, if a code is linked with the word \"with,\" it indicates an associated condition that presumes a relationship. Also, it is important to check if a second code is needed alongside the primary code, as combination codes typically encompass relevant details within a single code, implying a connection between the conditions involved (ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection).\n\nI could not cover the specific details of every code's function or the technicalities of each condition associated with combination codes. If you have questions about specific codes, please provide them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
